PDS-24 — Stage 2: Preprocessing

What this does:
Takes a raw dump of posts from any source and produces one clean, de-identified table for Stage 3:

    post_id | subreddit | cleaned_text | timestamp | metadata

Steps, in order:
1. Drop deleted / mod-removed posts, bot posts (AutoModerator) and rows with no ID.
2. Build the text as title + body, then de-identify: usernames (u/name, @name), emails, URLs and
   phone numbers become [USER], [EMAIL], [URL], [PHONE]. The author column is never written out.
   Limitation: names or places typed in the text itself are NOT removed.
3. Drop posts under MIN_WORDS words, and (optional, per source) posts that don't mention diabetes.
4. Deduplicate (same ID, or same text after normalising case/punctuation), keeping the earliest copy.
   This happens before any train/val/test split.
5. Record diabetes type and length in word pieces (all-mpnet-base-v2 tokenizer) in metadata.

metadata is a JSON string with: source, unit, parent_id, flair, diabetes_type, diabetes_type_source,
n_words, n_wordpieces. Use expand_metadata() from the script to turn it back into columns.

A <output>_report.json is written next to the output with how many rows each step dropped, the
diabetes type counts, and the share of posts over 384 word pieces (OPEN decision #7).


Setup:
    py -m pip install pandas
    py -m pip install transformers      (only needed for word-piece counts; or run with --skip-wordpieces)


Run (on the synthetic sample, from this folder):
    py stage2_preprocessing.py --input sample_data/sample_raw_posts.csv --source-config source_configs/sample.json

Options:
    --output                  default output/cleaned_posts.csv
    --diabetes-type-method    flair_then_keywords (default) | keywords | flair | subreddit | none
    --skip-wordpieces         skip the tokenizer download


Input Format / using a different data source:
The cleaning code never reads source-specific column names. Each source gets a small JSON file in
source_configs/ that maps its columns onto the standard raw columns:

    post_id (required), subreddit, title, body (at least one of title/body), timestamp, flair, author, parent_id

Source config keys:
    name              recorded in metadata.source
    format            csv | jsonl | json | parquet  (optional, otherwise taken from the file extension)
    unit              "post" or "comment"
    columns           standard column -> column name in the raw file
    constants         standard column -> fixed value (e.g. a subreddit name the raw file doesn't include)
    timestamp_unit    "s" or "ms" for epoch numbers, leave out for date strings
    id_prefix         added to every post_id so IDs stay unique if sources are merged
    relevance_filter  true to drop posts that don't mention diabetes (for non-diabetes communities)

Included configs:
    sample.json                  synthetic test file in sample_data/
    reddit_praw_prototype.json   output of data_collection/reddit_collection.py saved to CSV
    reddit_dump_ndjson.json      Pushshift / Arctic Shift style Reddit archive dumps

For a new database: copy one of these, change the column names, run. If the new source has text
that needs extra cleaning (HTML pages, quoted replies, signatures), add it in deidentify().


Still OPEN (not decided by this script, see pds24-handoff.md #10):
- Which communities count as "Asia-focused".
- Posts only vs posts + comments (set "unit" and map "parent_id" for comments).
- Source of diabetes type. Default is flair, then keyword rules; the method used is recorded per post
  in metadata.diabetes_type_source so it can be changed and re-run.
